      "semantic": {
        "text": "ModernWallet Free Financial Calculators Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Business Loan Payoff Personal Loan Interest Per Day Taxable vs. Tax-Deferred Credit Card Payoff IUL Merchant Cash Advance Invoice Factoring Business Line of Credit PTO Cash-Out Zakat Life Insurance Needs Self-Employment Tax Operating Cash Flow Mileage Deduction Cash Conversion Cycle Freelance Rate HELOC S-Corp Tax Commercial Mortgage CD Calculator Emergency Fund Debt Consolidation Inflation Expense Ratio Social Security Break-Even Mileage Reimbursement Portfolio Rebalancing Hurricane Deductible Annuity Credit Card Interest Loan Payment HYSA vs CD Trump Account vs 529 Mortgage Recast Social Security COLA Biweekly Mortgage Roth vs Traditional 401(k) Pay Off Mortgage or Invest Florida Amendment 3 Student Loan Garnishment Tax Bracket Debt-to-Income Ratio Taxable Social Security Checklists Charitable Donation Deduction Salary Car Loan Interest Deduction Paycheck Rent vs Buy Stock Capital Gains Tax Hidden Cost of Owning a Home Backdoor Roth Pro Rata × ModernWallet / Guides / I Bond Interest Rate: Current 5.51% Rate and How It Works I Bond Interest Rate: Current 5.51% Rate and How It Works By Jonathan Velez , Personal Finance Writer & Editor · Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 18, 2026 I bonds issued from November 1, 2026 to April 30, 2027 earn a 5.51% composite rate, which combines a permanent 1.40% fixed rate with a 2.04% semiannual inflation rate. This combined rate applies for the first six months after purchase, after which the inflation portion updates on a set schedule tied to your issue month. Tools for this journey Inflation → High-yield savings vs CD → The Current I Bond Rate The current I bond interest rate is 5.51% for bonds purchased between November 1, 2026 and April 30, 2027, based on published data retrieved from TreasuryDirect on November 18, 2026. This earnings rate, also called the composite rate or combined rate, consists of two separate components: a fixed rate of 1.40% and a semiannual inflation rate of 2.04%. The fixed rate never changes once you buy the bond. The inflation rate changes every six months, which means the composite rate can go up or down over time. Rate Component Published Rate Effective Window Notes Fixed Rate 1.40% Nov. 1, 2026 to Apr. 30, 2027 Remains the same for the full 30-year life of the bond Semiannual Inflation Rate 2.04% Nov. 1, 2026 to Apr. 30, 2027 Calculated from changes in the non-seasonally adjusted CPI-U Composite Rate (Earnings Rate) 5.51% First 6 months from purchase Combined annual rate applied to principal during the initial holding period The U.S. Department of the Treasury announces new rates on May 1 and November 1 of each year. The inflation component is set using changes in the non-seasonally adjusted Consumer Price Index for all Urban Consumers (CPI-U) for all items. When inflation rises, the composite rate increases. If deflation occurs, the semiannual inflation rate can become negative and reduce the composite rate below the fixed rate, but the composite rate stops at zero so your bond value does not drop. Interest on Series I savings bonds is earned from the first day of the month you complete the purchase. Twice a year the earned interest is added to the principal and the new rate applies to the larger value (semiannual compounding). From that point forward, the new composite rate applies to the larger combined balance. How the I Bond Composite Rate Is Calculated The composite rate is determined using a specific mathematical formula that blends the fixed rate and the semiannual inflation rate rather than simply adding them together. TreasuryDirect defines the composite rate formula as: Composite Rate = Fixed Rate + (2 x Semiannual Inflation Rate) + (Fixed Rate x Semiannual Inflation Rate) For bonds issued from November 1, 2026 to April 30, 2027, the inputs are a fixed rate of 1.40% (0.0140) and a semiannual inflation rate of 2.04% (0.0204). Plugging those exact values into the formula produces three components: Fixed rate: 0.0140 Twice the inflation rate: 2 x 0.0204 = 0.0408 The product of both rates: 0.0140 x 0.0204 = 0.0002856 Adding these three terms together yields 0.0140 + 0.0408 + 0.0002856 = 0.0550856. TreasuryDirect rounds the final result to two decimal places of a percent, which gives 0.0551, or 5.51%. In our internal calculation, we replayed TreasuryDirect's formula on its published inputs and reproduced the 5.51% rate, plus eight other rows of its current rate table. For instance, bonds with a 0.90% fixed rate paired with the 2.04% inflation rate compute to 0.0090 + 0.0408 + 0.0001836 = 0.0499836, which rounds to 5.00%. Bonds with a 1.10% fixed rate yield 5.20%, a 1.20% fixed rate yields 5.30%, and a 1.30% fixed rate yields 5.41%. In historical periods with different inputs, the formula behaved consistently. For the May 2022 rate period, a 0.00% fixed rate paired with a 4.81% semiannual inflation rate calculated to a 9.62% composite rate. For November 2022, a 0.40% fixed rate and a 3.24% inflation rate produced 6.89%. The formula also includes a zero floor for deflationary periods. If the semiannual inflation rate were negative 1.50% against a 1.40% fixed rate, the formula would yield 0.0140 - 0.0300 - 0.00021 = -0.01621. However, TreasuryDirect stops the composite rate at 0.00%, ensuring that your accumulated principal and previous interest are never diminished. If the semiannual inflation rate is exactly 0.00%, the composite rate equals the 1.40% fixed rate. You can evaluate broader purchasing power trends over time with our inflation calculator . When Your Own I Bond Rate Changes Your individual bond's interest rate does not update immediately when TreasuryDirect announces new rates on May 1 or November 1. Instead, the rate changes every six months based on the specific month you purchased the bond. Every Series I bond receives the published composite rate for six full calendar months from its issue month. When the semiannual period concludes, the bond resets to the latest composite rate available on that reset date, retaining its original fixed rate while adopting the current inflation component. Issue Month First Rate Reset Date Second Rate Reset Date January July 1 January 1 February August 1 February 1 March September 1 March 1 April October 1 April 1 May November 1 May 1 June December 1 June 1 July January 1 July 1 August February 1 August 1 September March 1 September 1 October April 1 October 1 November May 1 November 1 December June 1 December 1 For example, if you buy an I bond on January 15, 2027, your issue month is January 2027. You receive the 5.51% composite rate from January 1 through June 30. On July 1, 2027, your bond resets to the composite rate announced by the Treasury on May 1, 2027. You keep that new rate through December 31, 2027, before resetting again on January 1, 2028. Because of this delayed reset cycle, older I bonds currently earn different composite rates depending on when they were issued and what fixed rate they hold. For the six months running from November 2026 to April 2027, bonds bought between May 2026 and October 2026 earn 5.00%, bonds bought between November 2025 and April 2026 earn 5.00%, bonds from May 2025 to October 2025 earn 5.20%, and bonds from November 2024 to April 2025 earn 5.30%. Bonds bought between May 2024 and October 2024 earn 5.41%, while those purchased between November 2022 and April 2023 earn 4.49%. The highest rate currently paid on older bonds in the published table is 7.75% for bonds issued between May 2000 and October 2000, followed by 7.55% for bonds bought between November 1999 and April 2000 as well as November 2000 to April 2001. The Fixed Rate Is the Part You Keep The fixed rate remains locked for the entire 30-year term of the bond. While the inflation component fluctuates twice a year, the fixed rate lasts for the life of the bond. Between November 1, 2026 and April 30, 2027, the published fixed rate is 1.40%. This is an increase from the 0.90% fixed rate offered during the May 1, 2026 to October 31, 2026 window, as well as the 0.90% rate offered from November 1, 2025 to April 30, 2026. Prior six-month periods saw fixed rates of 1.10% (May 2025), 1.20% (November 2024), 1.30% (May 2024 and November 2023), 0.90% (May 2023), 0.40% (November 2022), and 0.00% (May 2022). Announcement Date Fixed Rate Semiannual Inflation Rate Initial Composite Rate November 1, 2026 1.40% 2.04% 5.51% May 1, 2026 0.90% 1.67% 4.26% November 1, 2022 0.40% 3.24% 6.89% May 1, 2022 0.00% 4.81% 9.62% For example, during the November 2026 to April 2027 cycle, a bond issued with a 0.90% fixed rate earns a 5.00% composite rate. A newly issued bond with a 1.40% fixed rate earns 5.51% during the same cycle. When evaluating fixed returns, compare how I bonds differ from other government securities. In our I bonds vs EE bonds comparison , Series EE bonds offer a fixed rate of 2.90% for issues between November 1, 2026 and April 30, 2027. Similarly, our I bonds vs TIPS comparison explains how Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities trade on the secondary market with price fluctuation risk, whereas I bonds protect your principal from market volatility. What $10,000 Earns in the First Year at 5.51% Under federal purchasing rules from TreasuryDirect , individuals can purchase up to $10,000 in electronic Series I savings bonds per calendar year per Social Security Number. Since January 1, 2025, savings bonds are issued in electronic form only through TreasuryDirect, with a $25 minimum purchase. If you invest the maximum $10,000 in an I bond issued at 5.51%, your earnings for the first six months are fixed by that initial composite rate. The annual 5.51% rate translates to a 2.755% return over the initial half-year. Applying that rate to the $10,000 principal yields exactly $275.50 in interest, bringing your bond balance to $10,275.50 after six months. What happens in months 7 through 12 depends entirely on the semiannual inflation rate announced on May 1, 2027. Your bond keeps its 1.40% fixed rate, but that fixed rate combines with the new inflation figure. Below is an illustration of what $10,000 earns over 12 months under three distinct inflation scenarios, assuming the annual composite divided by 2 is applied each six months with semiannual compounding and pre-tax terms: Scenario (Inflation Reset at Month 7) Months 1 to 6 Interest Month 7 to 12 Composite Rate Months 7 to 12 Interest Value After 12 Months Semiannual inflation resets to 0.00% $275.50 1.40% $71.93 $10,347.43 Semiannual inflation stays at 2.04% $275.50 5.51% $283.09 $10,558.59 Semiannual inflation resets to 4.00% $275.50 9.46% $486.03 $10,761.53 If the semiannual inflation rate falls to 0.00%, the composite rate drops to the 1.40% fixed rate, earning $71.93 on the $10,275.50 balance to reach $10,347.43. If the semiannual inflation rate repeats at 2.04%, the composite rate stays at 5.51%, generating $283.09 in second-half interest for a total value of $10,558.59. If inflation surges to 4.00%, the composite rate rises to 9.46% (0.0140 + 0.0800 + 0.00056 = 0.09456, rounding to 9.46%), producing $486.03 in interest and lifting the total value to $10,761.53. Liquidity rules dictate when you can access these earnings. You cannot cash in an I bond during the first 12 months after purchase. If you redeem a bond before holding it for 5 years, you forfeit the last 3 months of earned interest. For instance, cashing out at 18 months leaves you with 15 months of interest. Account values displayed in TreasuryDirect for bonds under five years old automatically deduct those three months of interest. For broader allocation guidance, review our analysis on whether are I bonds a good investment . Our Takeaway The 5.51% I bond rate suits money you can afford to leave untouched for at least one full year, within the $10,000 annual electronic purchase limit per person. The 1.40% fixed rate is the foundational asset you keep for 30 years, while the remainder adjusts with future inflation readings. In our internal calculation, we replayed TreasuryDirect's formula on its published inputs and reproduced the 5.51% composite rate along with eight other published rows. These calculations confirm how the fixed rate stabilizes the return even if inflation drops to zero. This investment is not suitable for everyone. It does not suit anyone who needs access to their cash inside 12 months, because federal rules completely prohibit redemption during the first year. It does not suit individuals looking to invest large sums above the annual $10,000 electronic purchase limit. It also does not serve anyone seeking an interest rate forecast, because future inflation readings and the upcoming May 1, 2027 announcement cannot be predicted in advance. Several factors would change our assessment of this rate. A lower composite rate announcement on May 1, 2027 would reduce future interest accruals. An immediate need for liquidity inside 12 months makes I bonds entirely inaccessible. Finally, finding a higher after-tax return elsewhere could make other options more attractive, especially after accounting for federal income tax. To manage your existing holdings, look up your own bond's issue month in the reset schedule above, then read the I bond interest rate directly in your TreasuryDirect account. Frequently asked questions What is the current interest rate on I bonds? The current interest rate on Series I savings bonds is 5.51% for bonds issued between November 1, 2026 and April 30, 2027. This rate includes a permanent 1.40% fixed rate and a 2.04% semiannual inflation rate. The rate applies for the first six months after you buy the bond. Is it better to buy EE or I savings bonds? Series I bonds currently offer a 5.51% composite rate that adjusts for inflation, while Series EE bonds issued between November 1, 2026 and April 30, 2027 offer a 2.90% fixed rate. You can read our detailed side-by-side comparison in I bonds vs EE bonds . Which bond is paying 7.5% interest? No Series I savings bond issued today pays 7.5% interest. In TreasuryDirect's current rate table, older I bonds bought between November 1999 and April 2000, as well as November 2000 to April 2001, earn a 7.55% composite rate, while bonds bought between May 2000 and October 2000 earn 7.75%. Which bonds give 12% return? No U.S. savings bond currently pays a 12% return. The current composite rate for new purchases through April 30, 2027 is 5.51%. How much is a $1,000 savings bond worth after 30 years? The exact value of a $1,000 savings bond after 30 years depends entirely on its issue date, bond series, and historical interest rate adjustments. TreasuryDirect does not publish a single value for all 30-year bonds because each issue period earns different rates over time. You can look up the value of a specific bond in your TreasuryDirect account or its Savings Bond Calculator. Are I bonds still earning interest? Yes, Series I savings bonds earn interest continuously for up to 30 years from their issue date, or until you cash them in. Twice a year the earned interest is added to the principal and the new rate applies to the larger value (semiannual compounding). Once an I bond reaches 30 years of age, it stops earning interest. When can I cash in an I bond? You can cash in a Series I savings bond after holding it for 12 months. If you redeem your bond before 5 years, you forfeit the last 3 months of interest as an early redemption penalty. Once you hold the bond for 5 years or longer, you can cash it out at any time without penalty. Is I bond interest taxable? Series I bond interest is subject to federal income tax, but it is exempt from all state and local income taxes according to TreasuryDirect tax guidelines . You can report the interest annually or defer reporting it until you cash the bond or it reaches maturity. Using bond proceeds for qualified higher education expenses may allow you to exclude the interest from federal income tax under specific TreasuryDirect higher education rules . Is this financial advice? No. This guide provides general educational information based on published data retrieved from TreasuryDirect on November 18, 2026, and does not constitute financial, investment, or tax advice. Series I savings bond rates are set by the U.S. Department of the Treasury and change on May 1 and November 1 of each year. Sources We prioritize primary sources for rules, formulas, rates, limits, and definitions. See our calculator methodology and editorial policy . TreasuryDirect I bonds interest rates TreasuryDirect I bonds TreasuryDirect buying savings bonds TreasuryDirect tax information for EE and I bonds TreasuryDirect using bonds for higher education ModernWallet Free, accurate financial calculators — auto loans, mortgages, retirement, investing, rentals, and net worth. No signup, instant results. 📣 Advertise With Us Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Business Loan Payoff Personal Loan Interest Per Day Taxable vs. Tax-Deferred Credit Card Payoff IUL Merchant Cash Advance Invoice Factoring Business Line of Credit PTO Cash-Out Zakat Life Insurance Needs Self-Employment Tax Operating Cash Flow Mileage Deduction Cash Conversion Cycle Freelance Rate HELOC S-Corp Tax Commercial Mortgage CD Calculator Emergency Fund Debt Consolidation Inflation Expense Ratio Social Security Break-Even Mileage Reimbursement Portfolio Rebalancing Hurricane Deductible Annuity Credit Card Interest Loan Payment HYSA vs CD Trump Account vs 529 Mortgage Recast Social Security COLA Biweekly Mortgage Roth vs Traditional 401(k) Pay Off Mortgage or Invest 