        "text": "ModernWallet Free Financial Calculators Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Business Loan Payoff Personal Loan Interest Per Day Taxable vs. Tax-Deferred Credit Card Payoff IUL Merchant Cash Advance Invoice Factoring Business Line of Credit PTO Cash-Out Zakat Life Insurance Needs Self-Employment Tax Operating Cash Flow Mileage Deduction Cash Conversion Cycle Freelance Rate HELOC S-Corp Tax Commercial Mortgage CD Calculator Emergency Fund Debt Consolidation Inflation Expense Ratio Social Security Break-Even Mileage Reimbursement Portfolio Rebalancing Hurricane Deductible Annuity Credit Card Interest Loan Payment HYSA vs CD Trump Account vs 529 Mortgage Recast Social Security COLA Biweekly Mortgage Roth vs Traditional 401(k) Pay Off Mortgage or Invest Florida Amendment 3 Student Loan Garnishment Tax Bracket Debt-to-Income Ratio Taxable Social Security Checklists Charitable Donation Deduction Salary Car Loan Interest Deduction Paycheck Rent vs Buy Stock Capital Gains Tax Hidden Cost of Owning a Home Backdoor Roth Pro Rata × ModernWallet / Guides / Are I Bonds a Good Investment in November 2026? Are I Bonds a Good Investment in November 2026? By Jonathan Velez , Personal Finance Writer & Editor · Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 18, 2026 I bonds are a good investment for cash you will not need for at least 12 months and want protected from inflation, up to $10,000 per person per calendar year. As of November 18, 2026, the Series I savings bond composite rate is 5.51% for bonds issued November 1, 2026 to April 30, 2027. That figure includes a 1.40% fixed rate that never changes for the life of the bond. If you need your cash inside a year or hold a savings account yielding more than the effective return after the early redemption penalty, a savings account is the better choice. Tools for this journey Inflation → High-yield savings vs CD → Who Should Buy I Bonds Right Now I bonds fit buyers who want to protect up to $10,000 against inflation and will not touch the principal for at least one full year. According to the TreasuryDirect I bonds page , Series I savings bonds issued from November 1, 2026 to April 30, 2027 pay a composite rate of 5.51%. That composite consists of two parts: a 1.40% fixed rate and a semiannual inflation rate of 2.04% announced on November 1, 2026. The 1.40% fixed rate never changes for as long as you own the bond. The inflation rate resets every six months based on inflation measurements, which means your combined earnings adjust twice a year. If deflation occurs, the combined rate stops at zero, so your rate never drops below zero percent. Your timeline and cash needs decide whether an I bond beats the alternatives. A high-yield savings account works better if you need liquidity before 12 months pass. Reader Situation Recommendation Primary Reason Money needed inside 12 months Skip I bonds TreasuryDirect rules strictly prohibit cashing in before 12 months Money set aside for 1 to 5 years Buy I bonds The 1.40% fixed rate stays permanent, though you forfeit the last 3 months of interest if redeemed before 5 years Cash above $10,000 per person Split between options Purchases are capped at $10,000 in electronic bonds per Social Security number per calendar year Investor seeking a permanent real return Buy I bonds The 1.40% fixed component remains attached to the bond for up to 30 years For investors who want an inflation-protected asset, marketable securities like Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities offer an alternative route. See our I bonds vs TIPS guide for how those instruments differ in liquidity and limits. If you want a fixed-rate government bond without inflation adjustments, compare choices in our I bonds vs EE bonds guide. I Bond Rules That Decide the Answer Five operational rules established by the United States Department of the Treasury determine whether Series I bonds work for your portfolio. You face an absolute 12-month lock. Under Treasury rules, you cannot cash in an I bond during the first 12 months after purchase. If an emergency occurs at month eight, your money remains completely unavailable in your account. Cashing out before five years triggers an interest penalty. If you redeem a bond before holding it for five years, you lose the last three months of interest. For example, if you cash in a bond at 18 months, TreasuryDirect pays you the first 15 months of interest and keeps the final three months. Values displayed for bonds under five years old inside your TreasuryDirect account already exclude those last three months of interest. Once you reach five years of ownership, the penalty disappears entirely. Annual purchases have a strict ceiling. You can buy up to $10,000 in electronic I bonds per calendar year per Social Security number. The minimum electronic purchase is $25, and you can buy in any amount to the penny above that threshold. Purchases are electronic only. Since January 1, 2025, purchases are electronic only through a TreasuryDirect account per the TreasuryDirect buying savings bonds page . For other savings bonds, the Series EE bond pays a 2.90% rate for bonds issued November 1, 2026 to April 30, 2027. Interest pays out at redemption. I bonds earn interest until you cash them in or until they reach their 30-year maturity date. You receive no regular cash distributions into a bank account along the way. All accumulated interest is paid when you redeem the bond or when the bond matures. I Bonds vs a High-Yield Savings Account Over 12 Months Headline yields do not tell the whole story because the three-month interest penalty reduces your real payout at month 12. In our internal modeling, we compared $10,000 in an I bond with $10,000 in a savings account over 12 months using Python calculations based on published rate formulas. Assuming an initial investment of $10,000 bought in November 2026, the bond earns the published 5.51% composite rate for the first six months, bringing the month-six principal to $10,275.50. The second-six-month composite rate resets on May 1, 2027, and its future value is an unknown variable. Because redeeming at month 12 causes the loss of the last three months of interest, you only collect nine months of total interest. The table below shows the calculated outcomes under three assumed second-half composite rates (1.0%, 3.0%, and 5.51% annualized) alongside savings accounts with assumed yields of 3.5%, 4.0%, and 4.5% Annual Percentage Yield (APY). Savings account yields and second-half I bond rates are labeled assumptions, not quotes or forecasts. Results are pre-tax and use simplified accrual, whereas TreasuryDirect account records are authoritative. Investment Option Assumed Rate Details Ending Balance at Month 12 12-Month Dollar Gain Effective 12-Month Return I Bond (Assumed 1.0% second half) 5.51% for 6 mos, 1.0% for 6 mos, minus 3 mos penalty $10,301.19 $301.19 3.01% I Bond (Assumed 3.0% second half) 5.51% for 6 mos, 3.0% for 6 mos, minus 3 mos penalty $10,352.57 $352.57 3.53% I Bond (Assumed 5.51% second half) 5.51% for 12 mos, minus 3 mos penalty $10,417.05 $417.05 4.17% Savings Account (Assumed 3.5% APY) Constant 3.5% APY with full liquidity $10,350.00 $350.00 3.50% Savings Account (Assumed 4.0% APY) Constant 4.0% APY with full liquidity $10,400.00 $400.00 4.00% Savings Account (Assumed 4.5% APY) Constant 4.5% APY with full liquidity $10,450.00 $450.00 4.50% Our modeling illustrates how the early redemption penalty drags down the headline 5.51% rate over a single year. If the second-half composite rate drops to 1.0%, an investor redeeming at month 12 earns an effective 3.01% return ($301.19), trailing all three assumed savings yields. Even if the composite rate stays at 5.51% through the May 1, 2027 reset, the three-month penalty caps the 12-month gain at $417.05, 