ts: The bonds must be Series EE or Series I savings bonds issued after 1989. The bonds must be registered in your name as the owner. If a child is listed as the owner, the exclusion cannot be claimed, so parents must retain ownership. You must have been 24 years of age or older before the issue date of the bonds. Your modified adjusted gross income must sit below the annual threshold limits established by the Internal Revenue Service. You must cash the bonds during the exact same tax year in which you pay qualified higher education expenses at an eligible educational institution for yourself, your spouse, or a dependent. Your tax filing status cannot be married filing separately. To claim the education exclusion, you file IRS Form 8815 alongside your federal tax return when reporting the redemption. When I Bonds Are the Wrong Tool I bonds are the wrong financial instrument in three common situations. Do not use I bonds for your core emergency fund. The 12-month lock means you cannot withdraw principal during the first year under any circumstances. If your car breaks down or an unexpected medical bill arrives in month six, those bond funds cannot help you. Before locking dollars away in a TreasuryDirect account, assess your liquid cash buffer by reading our guide on how much emergency fund you need in an immediately accessible account. Do not rely on I bonds if you need to park large sums of money above the annual ceiling. Because Treasury limits purchases to $10,000 per Social Security number each calendar year, you cannot place a larger balance in I bonds. For larger cash allocations that require stability and predictable yields, evaluate fixed-term bank products through our HYSA vs CD comparison. Do not choose I bonds when marketable government paper or bank deposits deliver higher after-tax yields. The TreasuryDirect TIPS comparison page notes that TIPS have a $100 minimum purchase and can be sold before maturity, whereas I bonds are non-marketable. To explore ho