urn after accounting for the three-month penalty. How are I bonds taxed? Interest earned on I bonds is subject to federal income tax, but it is completely exempt from state and local income taxes. You can report the interest annually or defer reporting until you redeem the bonds, and qualified higher education expenses may allow you to exclude the interest from federal tax using IRS Form 8815. Can I lose money in an I bond? No, you cannot lose principal in an I bond. TreasuryDirect rules set a floor at zero percent for the combined composite rate, meaning deflation will never reduce your bond value. However, cashing out before five years forfeits the last three months of interest earnings. When can I cash in an I bond? You can cash in a Series I bond after holding it for 12 months. If you redeem between 12 months and five years, you forfeit the last three months of interest. After five full years of ownership, you can redeem at any time with no penalty. Is this financial advice? No. This page provides general information based on TreasuryDirect pages retrieved on November 18, 2026, and does not constitute financial or tax advice. Interest rates for new Series I savings bonds reset twice a year, on May 1 and November 1. Sources We prioritize primary sources for rules, formulas, rates, limits, and definitions. See our calculator methodology and editorial policy . TreasuryDirect I bonds TreasuryDirect I bonds interest rates TreasuryDirect buying savings bonds TreasuryDirect tax information for EE and I bonds TreasuryDirect using bonds for higher education TreasuryDirect comparison of TIPS and I bonds ModernWallet Free, accurate financial calculators — auto loans, mortgages, retirement, investing, rentals, and net worth. No signup, instant results. 📣 Advertise With Us Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Bus